## 🔍 Findings

### Balanced weighting

| Rank | Platform | Score |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Google Gemini (Vertex AI) | 82.5 |
| 2 | OpenAI | 78.3 |
| 3 | Azure OpenAI (Microsoft Foundry) | 73.3 |
| 4 | Anthropic Claude (first-party) | 71.7 |

### The verdict flips, and that's the point

A comparison tool that returns the same winner no matter how you weight it isn't a decision tool, it's an advertisement. This one inverts under a model-risk weighting:

| Weighting | Winner | Runner-up |
|---|---|---|
| Balanced / PHI / EU / Public sector / Auditor | **Google** 82–87 | OpenAI or Azure |
| **Model risk** (injection 40, training 35) | **Anthropic** 85.7 | OpenAI 84.2 |

Single-criterion winners are split four ways — Google takes certification, residency and transparency; Anthropic takes training protection and injection resistance; OpenAI takes retention. There is no dominant platform, only a dominant answer *per risk posture*.

---

## 🧠 Key Learnings

**1. The most useful output was the eight cells I couldn't fill.**
I expected the deliverable to be a ranking. The actually valuable artefact was the list of questions four major AI vendors do not publicly answer:

- Anthropic ISO 27701 — not claimed anywhere I could find.
- Anthropic's subprocessor *names* — the DPA cites the URL correctly; the URL is a JavaScript trust portal that renders nothing.
- OpenAI FedRAMP High — doesn't exist; Moderate is the ceiling.
- OpenAI per-product ISO 42001 scope — company-level only, absent from the product matrix.
- Vertex AI abuse-monitoring retention window — docs JS-truncated, snippets conflict 30 vs 90 days, contract states no figure.
- Google quantitative injection metrics — four model cards, zero numbers.
- Azure OpenAI SOC 2 / ISO 27001 / 27701 / HIPAA **service-level** scope — Service Trust Portal login-gated.
- Azure OpenAI retention duration — **no published figure exists.**

**2. "Azure is SOC 2 certified" and "Azure OpenAI is SOC 2 certified" are different claims, and only one is publicly checkable.**
Certification granularity turned out to matter more than the checkmarks. Google publishes generative-AI-specific scope on every public certificate page. Microsoft publishes FedRAMP/DoD at the *service line-item level* — Azure OpenAI is explicitly listed through IL6, the strongest per-service evidence in the set — but hides SOC 2, ISO and HIPAA service scope behind a login. Anthropic and OpenAI gate the underlying reports entirely.

**3. A contractual prohibition is not the same instrument as a policy default.**
Anthropic's Commercial Terms say Anthropic *"may not train models on Customer Content"* — a bar. OpenAI says *"by default, we do not use your business data for training"* — a default. Google's Service Specific Terms §18 is a bar on Vertex; the free Gemini API is the opposite posture under the same brand. Azure qualifies its callout with *"without your permission or instruction."* Four different legal forms, all summarised in vendor marketing as "we don't train on your data." My rubric ranks bar > default > claim, and that single rule reorders the table.

**4. Defaults quietly undercut headline residency claims.**
Azure's Global Standard tier — Microsoft's *recommended* default — "may be processed in any Azure region," so residency is an opt-out. Anthropic's first-party API has no EU residency at all; the EU path runs through Bedrock/Vertex/Foundry, which changes who your data processor legally is. And Google's own residency commitment excludes Grounding with Search, Maps and RAG Engine, while recommending the `global` endpoint for latest features. In all three cases the compliant configuration is not the default configuration.

**5. Silence should score as risk, not as neutral.**
Azure OpenAI's retention score of 50 — the lowest cell in the matrix — reflects an absence, not a bad policy. The 30-day figure was in Microsoft's docs and was *removed* between Sept 2024 and Feb 2025; it now survives only as an employee forum post with no doc link. Most vendor scorecards would have left that cell blank or copied the stale number. Treating a withdrawn disclosure as a signal is the single most transferable idea from this build.

**6. Naming drift is a live failure mode for automated vendor-risk tooling.**
Vertex AI is now "Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform." Azure OpenAI's privacy doc is now "Models sold by Azure in Microsoft Foundry." Any script string-matching the old names against 2026 compliance scope lists returns false negatives — and would have told me Google's gen-AI platform wasn't in SOC 2 scope, when it's named explicitly.

